Track 2: Siete-vientos-drift
- Story & Context: In the shadow of the Port of Call, Maria whispered to the radio waves, admitting she was the one who sabotaged the main beacon to keep the lovers’ tugboat moored just offshore for one more hour. The concrete pier felt cold through her soles, but the warmth of the steel pan melody she improvised made her feel untouchable. "Do not let the tide decide our final sequence," she sang, her voice layered with the rhythmic, mechanical clicks of a drifting relay switch.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: A lonely figure sitting on a rusted iron bollard, wearing an iridescent poncho that reflects the amber glare of nearby harbor cranes, soft-focus industrial blur.

Track 3: The Great Cargo Ascension
- Story & Context: Centuries later, the workers still tell the myth of the vessel that didn't sail across the water but rose into the clouds, leaving nothing behind but a lingering, low-frequency oscillation that hums in the docks whenever the moon is full. Silas claimed to be the last man to see the hull glow, feeling the phantom vibrations of a thousand steel drums echoing in his very bones. "We are only the frequency waiting to be claimed by the sky," he recited, while the sound of the entire harbor seemed to rise up in a singular, dissonant chord.
